.TH "waybar\-backlight\-slider" "5" "2026\-08\-08"
.PP
.SH NAME
.PP
waybar \- backlight slider module
.PP
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The \fBbacklight slider\fR module displays and controls the current brightness of the default or preferred device.\&
.PP
The brightness can be controlled by dragging the slider across the bar or clicking on a specific position.\&
.PP
.SH CONFIGURATION
.PP
\fBmin\fR: 
.br
typeof: int 
.br
default: 0 
.br
The minimum volume value the slider should display and set.\&
.PP
\fBmax\fR: 
.br
typeof: int 
.br
default: 100 
.br
The maximum volume value the slider should display and set.\&
.PP
\fBorientation\fR: 
.br
typeof: string 
.br
default: horizontal 
.br
The orientation of the slider.\& Can be either `horizontal` or `vertical`.\&
.PP
\fBdevice\fR: 
.br
typeof: string 
.br
The name of the preferred device to control.\& If left empty, a device will be chosen automatically.\& 
.br
Both screen backlights (the udev \fBbacklight\fR subsystem) and keyboard backlights (LEDs in the udev \fBleds\fR subsystem, e.\&g.\& \fBwhite:kbd_backlight\fR) are supported; name such an LED here to control it.\& When left empty, a screen backlight is always preferred for automatic selection.\&
.PP
\fBinterval\fR: 
.br
typeof: uint 
.br
default: 1000 
.br
The interval in milliseconds at which the brightness is polled and the slider is updated.\&
.PP
\fBexpand\fR: 
.br
typeof: bool 
.br
default: false 
.br
Enables this module to consume all left over space dynamically.\&
.PP
\fBNote\fR: As well as the JSON configuration, the slider modules are special in
that they \fBrequire\fR styling to work.\& You \fBneed\fR to set \fBmin\-width\fR and/or
\fBmin\-height\fR (depending on whether your slider is vertical or not) for it to
display correctly.\& That is a GTK detail, not an issue with Waybar.\& See the
\fBSTYLE\fR section below.\&
.PP
\fBWarning\fR: If \fBmin\fR is set to \fB0\fR (default), the slider can set brightness to
\fB0\fR, which may completely disable the backlight on some devices, making the
screen fully black.\& Consider setting a small minimum value (e.\&g.\& \fB10\fR) or
configuring brightness keybinds as a fallback.\&
.PP
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
"modules\-right": [
    "backlight/slider",
],
"backlight/slider": {
    "min": 0,
    "max": 100,
    "orientation": "horizontal",
    "device": "intel_backlight"
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
.SH STYLE
.PP
The slider is a component with multiple CSS Nodes, of which the following are exposed:
.PP
\fB#backlight\-slider\fR: 
.br
Controls the style of the box \fBaround\fR the slider and bar.\&
.PP
\fB#backlight\-slider slider\fR: 
.br
Controls the style of the slider handle.\&
.PP
\fB#backlight\-slider trough\fR: 
.br
Controls the style of the part of the bar that has not been filled.\&
.PP
\fB#backlight\-slider highlight\fR: 
.br
Controls the style of the part of the bar that has been filled.\&
.PP
.SS STYLE EXAMPLE
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
#backlight\-slider slider {
    min\-height: 0px;
    min\-width: 0px;
    opacity: 0;
    background\-image: none;
    border: none;
    box\-shadow: none;
}

#backlight\-slider trough {
    min\-height: 80px;
    min\-width: 10px;
    border\-radius: 5px;
    background\-color: black;
}

#backlight\-slider highlight {
    min\-width: 10px;
    border\-radius: 5px;
    background\-color: red;
}
.fi
.RE
